# Story Plain Prose

Ordinary words in `Story/Chapters/` and in chat. Straight and logical. The story is the author's.

## Story

- Never paste reference shorthand, note labels, slang, or wiki jargon into the story.
- Keep coin math and craft facts in words Roland would think. Leave sheet terms in Notes / References.
- If a fact must land in prose, say it in ordinary language. Do not import the sheet name for it.
- State what a thing **is**, not that it "came back," "turned out," or "proved" to be that way, unless the plot is the change. The cloth **was** light and cool. Not "came back light and cool."
- **No double beats.** Do not put the same fact or beat in twice (commission and "after getting," summary and detail, and so on). Say it once. When their new line covers a fact, strip that fact from the leftover rather than leaving both.

## Seating

- Never say seat, seats, seating, seat-time, knows seating, or any Notes quality-only ability talk in story or chat.
- Say he can draw reliably, make them reliably, or reliably make.
- Leave seating wording in Notes and References only.

## Chat

- No auctioneer, trader, gamer, or wiki voice. Banned examples include hammers, flood, take-home, crossover, soft lock, volume stretch, side grind, and similar shop talk unless they typed that exact word into a file order.
- Name the real thing (sale price, coin after the house cut, materials cost, profit per scroll, scrolls per week).
- No sheet tags in answers (`L5+`, activations, cum) when ordinary words work. Say the skill name, the level, and whether the number was calculated or not.
- Answer the question. Do not teach extra system rules they did not ask for. Do not end with an offer to do more work.

## Teaching ban

- Never explain, justify, teach, or clarify in story unless they explicitly ordered that beat. No "so that," recipe recaps, or why-he-does-not-buy-X talk.
- Do not add "why this matters" or background the beat does not need. The author and reader already know.
